A general diode article should not be confused with the underlying electronic structure of semiconductor materials as a whole, nor with the specialized designs of individual power or optoelectronic components.

== The circuit and physical model are different ==

In the idealized low-level-injection diffusion model of a p–n junction, current is often approximated by

<math display="block">I=I_S\left[\exp\left(\frac{qV}{k_BT}\right)-1\right],</math>

with an effective reverse-saturation current scale <math>I_S</math>, elementary charge <math>q</math>, temperature <math>T</math> and Boltzmann constant <math>k_B</math>. This is not the complete behavior of real semiconductor diodes: series resistance, recombination, high injection, breakdown, parasitic capacitances and temperature effects change the terminal characteristic.<ref name="shockley_core">William Shockley, [https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1949.tb03645.x “The Theory of p-n Junctions in Semiconductors and p-n Junction Transistors”], ''Bell System Technical Journal'' 28, 435–489 (1949), original analytical junction treatment.</ref><ref name="mit_core">MIT OpenCourseWare, [https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-720j-integrated-microelectronic-devices-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/ ''Integrated Microelectronic Devices''], p–n junction and Schottky contact lecture sequence, including nonideal dynamics.</ref>

Different diode mechanisms demand different models; fitting one exponential to every diode is not sound physical inference. A measured I–V curve should record polarity, temperature, sweep speed, compliance limits and possible self-heating or hysteresis.

== Physical design and validation ==

Design considerations include forward voltage and loss at a specified operating current; maximum repetitive reverse voltage; switching/recovery time; reverse leakage; junction or depletion capacitance; peak surge current; thermal resistance; package limitations and intended frequency. Voltage alone is not enough: maximum current, power dissipation and transient energy also constrain safe operation.

Diodes are connected to [[Related to::Voltage|voltage]], [[Related to::Electric current|electric current]], [[Related to::Resistor|resistive circuit elements]] and [[Related to::Energy conversion|energy conversion]]. The [[Related to::Transistor|transistor]] is a related but distinct controllable electronic device. These links are explicit general semantic relationships, not declarations of subcomponent identity.

